\paragraph{Causal localization (Llama).}
In Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, causal influence is consistently localized to L13 (41\% depth) regardless of where detection accuracy peaks (EN$\to$EN at L28, ZH$\to$ZH at L13): late layers encode the outcome in a linearly readable form, while mid-layers host the computation that determines it.
An independent replication with a separately trained probe (seed=123, different train/test split) confirms the L13 causal effect: 4/6 $\alpha$ configurations survive Bonferroni correction (vs.\ 2/6 in the original), with all tested $\alpha$ values reaching $p \leq 0.004$ (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_replication}).

\section{Seed Replication of L13 Steering}
\label{app:seed_replication}

To verify that the L13 causal effect is not an artifact of a specific train/test split, we repeated the Llama L13 steering experiment with an independently trained probe (seed=123, different train/test split).
The causal effect fully replicates: $\alpha = +5$ ($p = 0.002$), $\alpha = +10$ ($p = 0.0005$), $\alpha = +20$ ($p = 0.004$), with 4 of 6 configurations surviving Bonferroni correction (vs.\ 2/6 in the original).
The consistent positive-direction effect across independent probe training confirms that L13 causal localization is not an artifact of a specific train/test split.

